Discussion. In contrast to Olivry et al.'s cost model, the equations produced by XNNC take into consideration the fact that the tiling window might not be a perfect divisor of the loop's iteration space. If tiling does not perfectly divide the iteration space, then the XNNC compiler generates epilogue code to fetch data outside the tiled loop. Consequently, the performance model contains conditionals. For instance, Figure 14 (a) was produced by a (simplified) equation like $\mathrm{c o s t}=C_{1}+\left( \mathrm{i f W\% 6 3 t h e n C_{2} e l s e 0}\right)+C_{3}\times\left( H/6 4\right).$ The coefficients $C_{i}$ represent costs of particular instructions; W and H are tile sizes. Due to these conditionals, the cost models are represented by discontinuous functions. In this case, the droplet expectation does not hold. However, if we restrict valid neighborhoods to only perfect divisors of the iteration space, then the Droplet Expectation holds. For instance, starting with tiling windows with size 16 or greater, coordinate descent achieves the optimal configuration in any of the surfaces seen in Figure 14 after three iterations. Notice that this restriction is unnecessary if some loss is acceptable. When applied to large deep-learning networks—under the same XNNC analytical model—Droplet Search stays very close to the global optimum, although sampling less than 1% of the space covered by an exhaustive grid search.

